course: dictionary
lesson: 957
title: Slang for being drunk
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Blotto» is slang. It is informal, old-fashioned British talk, and it means very drunk. Slang words are not used in school writing, but you may meet them in comic stories and old books.
explain: Context shows how strong a word is. «Merry» and «squiffy» mean a little drunk. «Blotto» and «three sheets to the wind» mean very drunk. Compare the words around each one to work out which is stronger.
explain: Being drunk is dangerous, and alcohol is not for children. In a safety lesson, the jokes in slang words do not make it safe. If an adult or a friend has had too much to drink, tell a trusted grown-up straight away.
fact: «Blotto» is old British slang for very drunk.
fact: «Merry» and «squiffy» mean a little drunk.
fact: «Blotto» and «three sheets to the wind» mean very drunk.
fact: Slang is informal talk, and some slang is old-fashioned.
fact: Alcohol is not for children, and being drunk can be dangerous.
exercise: «Blotto» means which of these? | *Very drunk | Very happy | Very hungry | Very tired
exercise: «Merry» and «squiffy» mean a little drunk. Which word means very drunk? | *Blotto | Squiffy | Tipsy and cheerful | Merry
exercise: Is alcohol safe for children to drink? | Yes, if an adult says so | Yes, a little is fine | Only on birthdays | *No, alcohol is not for children
test: Which word means a little drunk? | Legless | Three sheets to the wind | Blotto | *Merry
test: «Blotto» is old British slang. What kind of word is it? | A cosy word for a drink | A word from Scotland | *Old-fashioned slang | A medical term
exam: In a safety lesson, why is being blotto dangerous? | It makes you very clever | *It can lead to harm, and alcohol is not for children | It is good for your health | It makes you grow taller
